package dapr-wasm-components:interfaces@0.5.0;

/// Shared types used by all Dapr building-block interfaces.
///
/// ## Error model
///
/// There is deliberately no shared, all-encompassing `error` type. Failures
/// are sorted by *who acts on them*:
///
///  - **Expected outcomes** the caller branches on as normal control flow are
///    modelled in the *success* type — a missing value is `none` (not an
///    error), a rejected conditional write is a dedicated outcome, etc.
///  - **Recoverable failures** (setup/config such as "no such store" or
///    "permission denied", and the few operation-specific rejections like an
///    etag conflict) are modelled as per-interface and per-function `variant`
///    error types, so each function advertises only the failures it can
///    actually produce.
///  - **Unrecoverable failures** (the sidecar is unreachable or not ready, an
///    internal sidecar/component error, a timeout, an I/O fault) are *not*
///    represented in any type. A provider signals them by **trapping** — the
///    component-model equivalent of a Rust `panic!` / an unchecked exception —
///    which propagates to the caller as an aborted call. The application does
///    not (and cannot meaningfully) branch on them per operation; retries and
///    circuit-breaking belong to Dapr's resiliency policies or the host.
interface types {
  /// Free-form string key/value pairs. Used both for Dapr `metadata.*`
  /// request options and for response metadata/headers.
  type metadata = list<tuple<string, string>>;

  /// A JSON document, kept as a string. Used as an escape hatch where the
  /// Dapr API takes or returns arbitrary JSON.
  type json = string;

  /// A failure reported *by the application* from an inbound callback (e.g.
  /// it could not handle a delivered event). The sidecar's reaction
  /// (retry/drop/log) is component-specific. Trapping (panicking) from a
  /// callback is also a failure — use this type only to fail gracefully
  /// without aborting the instance.
  record app-error {
    message: string,
  }
}
